{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763821Z","id":"40f2b80d00337d9df0b8acba033a03e3eb0a2039c9ea168e2322bf627c88677e","body":{"detail":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:483 adds `Path::with_parent(self, parent: Parent)` which sets `path.parent`, the lineage pointer to a step in another document. crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:567 already has `Step::with_parent(self, parent: impl Into<String>)`, which pushes a step id onto `step.parents` (the DAG edge). Both are reachable as `.with_parent(...)` in the same builder chains (crates/toolpath/README.md:48-69 shows both styles), and 0.8.0 makes the new name public API. The two concepts are deliberately distinct in the RFC (\"Path Parent\" vs `step.parents`), so one verb for both invites `step.with_parent(...)`/`path.with_parent(...)` mix-ups and makes the docs harder to search.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":483},"end":{"line":486},"content_hash":"07cac37e42fd8f773cfb77a288abed8c7b27832a48c5938c39244a54eb9d7465"},"suggested_fix":"Name the path builder after the concept, e.g. `Path::continuing(parent)` or `Path::with_lineage(parent)`, before 0.8.0 is published; or at least cross-reference the two in their doc comments.","summary":"Path::with_parent and Step::with_parent share a name but mean different things","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925326Z","id":"0a9c63b22c3fdc2628a1b371e86fd5fd0af589d70c91102827d6306d950bd11d","body":{"detail":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:482-486: `pub fn with_parent(mut self, parent: Parent) -> Self { self.path.parent = Some(parent); ... }` on `Path`. crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:566-569: `pub fn with_parent(mut self, parent: impl Into<String>) -> Self { self.step.parents.push(parent.into()); ... }` on `Step`. crates/toolpath/README.md:49-50 and :63-69 show both in adjacent examples. The parameter types differ, so a mix-up is a compile error rather than a silent bug, which is why `suggestion` is the right kind; the naming claim itself holds and 0.8.0 is where the name becomes public API (CHANGELOG.md:12-13).","kind":"comment","references":"40f2b80d00337d9df0b8acba033a03e3eb0a2039c9ea168e2322bf627c88677e","summary":"confirmed: Path::with_parent (lineage) and Step::with_parent (DAG edge) share a name for different concepts","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:09:12.051738Z","id":"39d2bfcb0a973fe0da746c2f19b3f5a1d30ce372380ac90338b4cb94e4fa94ca","body":{"detail":"`Parent` in crates/toolpath/src/types.rs derives Deserialize without `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`, but `$defs/parent` in crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json:338-362 sets `additionalProperties: false`. Checked with a scratch binary against this crate: `serde_json::from_str::<Parent>(r#\"{\"uri\":\"u\",\"path\":\"p\",\"step\":\"s\",\"relation\":\"fork\"}\"#)` is Ok, and a .path.jsonl whose PathOpen carries that parent reads fine and writes back without `relation`. `path p validate` (crates/path-cli/src/cmd_validate.rs:16-22) runs the schema only for canonical JSON, so the same parent passes in a .path.jsonl file and fails in a .path.json file. `relation` is exactly the key that earlier drafts of this design wrote (see the dropped-relation alternative recorded on docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md). A writer that sets `relation: \"fork\"` with `step` equal to the parent's head means a fork, but every reader will silently classify the path as a continuation, and nothing reports it. Parent is a new type with no published documents, so making it strict breaks no existing data. It has no `#[serde(flatten)]` field, so deny_unknown_fields works on it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":139},"end":{"line":148},"content_hash":"0b7a93d6a1d2585dfcc580320e54595ab0359e713f069fe3a3b635e08e314065"},"suggested_fix":"Add `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` to `Parent`, and add a case to `test_parent_fields_required` (or a sibling test) asserting that `{\"uri\":..,\"path\":..,\"step\":..,\"relation\":\"fork\"}` is rejected. If leniency is intended instead, document it on `Parent` and add a test that pins the drop.","summary":"Parent accepts and silently drops unknown keys such as a stray \"relation\", while the JSON schema rejects them, so the same parent is valid as .path.jsonl and invalid as .path.json","tags":["review","review:pr-354-qual-2","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:02.179261Z","id":"44dbd091cd369babfedca550f97d4628d9d777587ed5a427190ee1b41cf32445","body":{"detail":"crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:139-148 derives Deserialize on Parent with no #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] (grep for deny_unknown_fields in crates/toolpath/src finds no use in code); serde ignores unknown fields by default (https://serde.rs/container-attrs.html#deny_unknown_fields). crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json:338-362 has additionalProperties:false. Scratch binary against this crate: serde_json::from_str::<Parent>(r#\"{\"uri\":\"u\",\"path\":\"p\",\"step\":\"s\",\"relation\":\"fork\"}\"#) => Ok, re-serialized as {\"uri\":\"u\",\"path\":\"p\",\"step\":\"s\"} (relation dropped). crates/path-cli/src/cmd_validate.rs:16-22 runs schema::validate only when !is_path_jsonl, so .path.jsonl skips the schema. relation is the key the dropped alternative 1840d8eb20d1c47032016aee598f34055d6f7549c094eccb80d0aba2dd1d7d3e names. Note that no other toolpath type uses deny_unknown_fields either, so this mismatch is crate-wide; concern (a risk, not a failure before merge) is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"39d2bfcb0a973fe0da746c2f19b3f5a1d30ce372380ac90338b4cb94e4fa94ca","summary":"confirmed: Parent silently drops unknown keys while $defs/parent sets additionalProperties:false","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
